.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1215 PM PDT Thu Sep 10 2026
(This evening through Friday night)

After a warm start to the day, the interior will remain very warm
to hot with the ongoing Heat Advisory still in place until 10 PM
PDT this evening. However, we are already seeing cooler conditions
near the coast as a shallow marine layer is forecast to return as
well as an increase in onshore winds.

Tonight, temperatures are forecast to cool into the mid 50s to
near 60 deg F near the coast and across the interior valleys.
However, the higher elevations look to only cool into the 70s and
potentially the lower 80s. Coastal stratus and/or fog look to
develop along the coast and potentially into the North Bay Valleys
overnight as the marine layer deepens to around 500 feet.

On Friday, depending on how much stratus/fog develops in the
morning, the cooling trend will continue with temperates
moderating to near seasonal averages with mostly sunny conditions
across the interior. This is a result of the mid/upper level
through deepening off of the Pacific Northwest coast and high
pressure over the Desert Southwest being suppressed to the south.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1215 PM PDT Thu Sep 10 2026
(Saturday through next Wednesday)

The gradual cooling trend will continue into the weekend as the
low pressure off of the Pacific Northwest coast deepens further,
bringing rainfall to that region. This will allow the marine layer
to deepen as well, reaching around 1500 feet by Saturday night
into Sunday. This would also bring increased afternoon and evening
winds in the favored valleys, peaks, passes, and gaps.

Beyond this weekend, troughing off of the West Coast looks likely
to persist. This will allow for afternoon maximum temperatures to
be near to slightly below average and overnight minimum
temperatures to be near or slightly above.

